    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d709">

                <pb facs="0747" n="489" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1981</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-27T13:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 27, 1915, 1 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11.40 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2536. The public and private and official and unofficial comment on our latest
                    note to Germany is practically unanimous in approval. Most of it is of very
                    hearty commendation. The very general opinion, as far as I can gather it, is
                    that war is inevitable between the United States and Germany. Nobody seems to
                    believe that Germany will meet our demands nor that she will cease her unlawful
                    submarine acts.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
